## The Case Studies: A Journey Through the Robots

The core thesis of this architecture is actively demonstrated across three operational robots, each exploring the agent framework at a progressively deeper level.

```
[ farm_soil ] ──> [ rancho_water ] ──> [ irrigation_analytics ]
(Consolidation)      (The Adapter)        (The Full Virtual Operator)

```

### 1. [farm_soil](https://www.google.com/search?q=%23) — Consolidation

* **Focus:** Pure ingest, observation, and moisture data aggregation.
* **Scope:** Its primary function is to pull and consolidate all fragmented soil moisture sources—including a LoRaWAN sensor console and two distinct ETo feeds—into a single, comprehensive daily operational picture. By unifying these disparate moisture metrics, it eliminates the need for growers to log into multiple visualization screens to check field conditions.

### 2. [rancho_water](https://www.google.com/search?q=%23) — The Adapter

* **Focus:** Minimally invasive integration and consumption monitoring.
* **Scope:** Operates against a single water district portal with no physical actuation capabilities. Its objective is to closely monitor daily water consumption. This monitoring is critical due to the soaring operational cost of water and the strict necessity of avoiding overdrawing the farm's legal or seasonal water allotment. Its structural engineering purpose is to prove the universal-adapter inversion—and test its resilience against UI brittleness—in a low-stakes environment before scaling.

### 3. [irrigation_analytics](https://www.google.com/search?q=%23) — The Full Virtual Operator

* **Focus:** End-to-end closed-loop automation.
* **Scope:** The definitive realization of the project's thesis. It exercises all six core operational themes: universal extraction, armed physical control, physics-grounded safety detectors, and an interactive analyst module that growers can actively interrogate.

*It is recommended to explore these system case studies in order, as each escalates from simple data consolidation toward a fully supervised, autonomous virtual operator.*


